1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:08,700
AirPatch lets Apertus propose code fixes and checks every proposal offline, in isolated containers, against hidden tests.

2
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:17,700
To use a sovereign model for code, you must measure whether its changes work - privately and without trusting it.

3
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:26,700
Seven small steps, each in its own container. The model only writes text; deterministic code decides what passes.

4
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:35,700
We attack our own verifier. The probe finds nothing - and the positive control shows it would notice a leak.

5
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:44,700
Forty new test tasks, a frozen pre-registered plan, three model conditions and four model-free baselines.

6
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:53,700
A weak, negative result, reported as measured: each model solved 2 of 40 tasks with one answer; repairs fixed none.

7
00:00:54,300 --> 00:01:02,700
The verifier showed the failing example and the model's own output - most repair answers simply repeated the previous answer.

8
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:11,700
Every answer is recorded. 'make run' without a key replays them offline and is labelled REPLAY everywhere.

9
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:20,700
We report the defect openly: found after the freeze, verified not to affect the results, fixed without touching them.

10
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:29,700
Docker and make are all you need. AirPatch: measure what a sovereign model writes - offline and reproducibly.
